 Ambrose Paré.

249
00:21:21,120 --> 00:21:24,570
 Well, Paré, you did just the right thing, and you're curing the men.

250
00:21:24,570 --> 00:21:26,800
 I treat them. God alone cures them.

251
00:21:27,670 --> 00:21:30,810
 I promote you to the rank of Chief Barber Surgeon of the Regiment.

252
00:21:34,050 --> 00:21:37,790
 Chief, sir? He disobeyed! He has to get punished!

253
00:21:38,490 --> 00:21:39,990
 You're demoted to assistant.

254
00:21:40,690 --> 00:21:43,080
 Now on you take orders from Ambrose Parade.

255
00:21:46,010 --> 00:21:49,880
 The soldier has an amputation.

256
00:21:51,180 --> 00:21:53,730
 Cauterize his wound with a hot eye. It's really inhuman.

257
00:21:54,050 --> 00:21:56,730
 Yes, but if we don't cauterize, he'll bleed to death.

258
00:21:57,120 --> 00:21:59,460
 Blood flows through vessels that are as big as that.

259
00:21:59,960 --> 00:22:01,730
 They could easily be sewn together.

260
00:22:02,250 --> 00:22:04,530
 Sewn together? You're joking, Ambrose.

261
00:22:05,410 --> 00:22:07,550
 No, in fact, I've already done it.

262
00:22:08,820 --> 00:22:10,750
 Such a thing to a man?

263
00:22:12,010 --> 00:22:15,280
 This dog's leg was blown away by a bomb.

264
00:22:15,810 --> 00:22:19,450
 I just cleaned out the stump and sewed up the wound.

265
00:22:21,090 --> 00:22:21,670
 Cauterize it?

266
00:22:22,410 --> 00:22:22,950
 Mm-mm.

267
00:22:23,670 --> 00:22:26,560
 We'll try a test.

268
00:22:26,830 --> 00:22:27,750
 It could work.

269
00:22:30,080 --> 00:22:34,880
 Good work, Doctor. My leg doesn't hurt me anymore.

270
00:22:35,680 --> 00:22:39,140
 I am not a doctor, my friend. No, I'm a barber-surgeon.

271
00:22:39,430 --> 00:22:42,810
 Oh, well, I'd say you're a better doctor than any doctor.

272
00:22:43,370 --> 00:22:51,320
 An idea. All right, stand up.

273
00:22:52,020 --> 00:22:53,300
 You think I...

274
00:22:53,300 --> 00:22:58,000
 Mm-hmm. Give me your crutches.

275
00:22:58,420 --> 00:22:59,380
 You sure about this?

276
00:23:01,810 --> 00:23:07,030
 Can't believe it. Is it true?

277
00:23:07,340 --> 00:23:08,980
 I'm walking by myself.

278
00:23:09,980 --> 00:23:11,860
 Oh, doctor, you've made a miracle.

279
00:23:12,160 --> 00:23:13,100
 It's a real miracle.

280
00:23:13,220 --> 00:23:14,000
 I'm going to show everybody.

281
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:14,580
 Just a minute.

282
00:23:14,740 --> 00:23:15,260
 Sit down.

283
00:23:15,760 --> 00:23:16,760
 I have another idea.

284
00:23:22,880 --> 00:23:23,980
 Golly, it's the best yet.

285
00:23:45,100 --> 00:23:46,460
 Wonderful things about your work.

286
00:23:46,900 --> 00:23:48,760
 Would you like to return and work here?

287
00:23:48,990 --> 00:23:50,030
 Yes, Mr. Curtin.

288
00:23:50,260 --> 00:23:52,290
 There is so much I still want to learn.

289
00:23:53,420 --> 00:23:55,300
 Paré was soon equal to the greatest.

290
00:23:55,900 --> 00:23:59,240
 Pioneer in experimental medicine, accomplished surgeon.

291
00:23:59,950 --> 00:24:02,250
 He wanted to share his knowledge with everyone.

292
00:24:02,250 --> 00:24:03,850
 This is intolerable.

293
00:24:05,020 --> 00:24:05,840
 This barber.

294
00:24:07,370 --> 00:24:08,750
 This Ambrose Paré.

295
00:24:09,030 --> 00:24:11,210
 Now he's published a learned treatise.

296
00:24:11,700 --> 00:24:13,060
 And in French, not in Latin.

297
00:24:14,260 --> 00:24:17,060
 Next, any charlatan will come and claim to know our art.

298
00:24:17,390 --> 00:24:19,380
 It's an insult to our profession.

299
00:24:21,190 --> 00:24:21,770
 It's true.

300
00:24:22,330 --> 00:24:23,330
 It is a scandal.

301
00:24:23,730 --> 00:24:26,570
 He dares suggest that he can stitch up blood vessels.

302
00:24:26,960 --> 00:24:30,010
 We have been cauterizing for five centuries with success.

303
00:24:30,320 --> 00:24:31,290
 We must act.

304
00:24:31,620 --> 00:24:33,130
 That's right. Prosecure him.

305
00:24:33,130 --> 00:24:35,530
 And what was the result of all this uproar?

306
00:24:36,540 --> 00:24:37,020
 Ambrose.

307
00:24:38,430 --> 00:24:41,700
 King Henry has appointed you his surgeon.

308
00:24:41,980 --> 00:24:45,300
 You realize you're a lowly kitchen boy surgeon to the king?

309
00:24:45,300 --> 00:24:50,010
 Yes, Ambrose was soon famous, and he made many, many more discoveries.

310
00:24:50,010 --> 00:24:54,190
 Imagine, antiseptics before man even knew about microbes.

311
00:24:54,190 --> 00:24:56,890
 He was truly the father of modern medicine.

312
00:24:56,890 --> 00:25:03,840
 A few decades after Parry's death, another great doctor, Harvey, would discover the way blood circulates.

313
00:25:03,840 --> 00:25:07,480
 But as always, there were fools in the way of progress.

314
00:25:07,480 --> 00:25:12,360
 Gentlemen, the so-called bloodstream that Harvey says exists is ridiculous.

315
00:25:12,360 --> 00:25:20,360
 This is paradoxical, useless to medicine, wrong, impossible, unintelligent, absurd, and I'd even say harmful to humans.

316
00:25:20,360 --> 00:25:26,110
 The learned doctor you just heard saying that famous sentence is indeed Guy Patin,

317
00:25:26,550 --> 00:25:31,450
 who was none other than the dean of the Paris Faculty of Medicine at the time.

318
00:25:32,270 --> 00:25:37,370
 And there we have proof once more that it's never easy to be a discoverer.

319
00:25:45,440 --> 00:26:06,240
 *Muchas music*

